X
XHere's a bit of a hack for those of you running OpenWindows or X11 on
Xa Sun who want the Num_Lock key to give the expected behaviour. In the
Xappended uuencoded compressed tar file there is a csh script called
X"xnumlock" that toggles between "xmodmap" reads of the two supplied files
X"numlock_on.map" and "numlock_off.map". Unfortunately, two invocations
Xof "xmodmap" seem to be required: one to get the current state and the
Xother to toggle it. This makes the script a bit slow (1-2 seconds on an
XIPX), but it's worth it. If anyone can figure out a faster way to do
Xthis, please post!
X
XThose of you lucky enough to be using tcsh and xterms can actually program
Xthe Num_Lock key to invoke xnumlock automatically! (What a concept!...)
XFirst you need a "dummy" keysym for the Num_Lock key, one that gives a
Xrecognizable code to tcsh. One example would be the "F" keys, say "F2".
XSo in your .xinitrc put in a line like:
X
X   xmodmap -e "keysym Num_Lock = F2"
X
Xthen somewhere in your .tcshrc file:
X
X   bindkey -s "^[[12~" "xnumlock\n"
X
X(assuming xnumlock is in your path). Unfortunately, OW cmdtools seem to
Xignore the F keys, but it will work in an xterm. Give it a try!
X
XThis version of "xnumlock" replaces the earlier version I posted on
Xcomp.sources.games.bugs, which altered the numeric keypad behaviour for
Xthe duration of the session. The new version lets you toggle back and
Xforth. Note that as before, xnumlock immediately affects all xterms and
X"true" X applications, but not OW applications. For the latter (e.g.
Xcmdtools), you must invoke xnumlock BEFORE starting the application.
X
XTo unpack this post, extract to a file called xnumlock.tar.Z.uu (for
Xexample) and type:
X
X   uudecode xnumlock.tar.Z.uu
X   zcat xnumlock.tar.Z | tar xvf -
X
XNote that the "map_path" variable defined at the beginning of the script
Xshould be set to wherever you put the two .map files.
X
XDerek
